* START-DESCRIPTION:
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account <ADD/DELETE> user.name
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SSH               - ssh only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account API               - the API only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account BOTH              - ssh and the API
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account NONE              - neither
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SH-ON | SH-OFF    - the SH verb and "!"
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account OS-ON | OS-OFF    - OS.EXECUTE and the screen editors
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SUSPENDED | UNSUSPENDED
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE TIERS ARE GONE, AND THESE ARE
*   THE TWO STATE FORMS THAT REMAIN.  SUSPENDED writes SUSPENDED to
*   ACC$SUSPENDED (field 5) and UNSUSPENDED clears it; nothing else in the
*   account moves either way - no Windows membership, no os.users record, no
*   VOC change, because every account's VOC is the whole of NEWVOC whatever the
*   field says.
*
* SUSPENDED DENIES ACCESS AND CHANGES NOTHING ELSE.  No Windows group
*   membership moves and the VOC is left exactly as it is, which is what makes
*   lifting one free.  Three doors refuse a suspended account: LOGIN, CPROC's
*   logto.authorised and APISRVR's vb.account.
*
* THE KEYWORD IS ABSOLUTE.  It says what the account's remote access is, and
* whatever it does not name is withdrawn - so API takes ssh away.  The same
* four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so access is said one way whether an account
* is being made or changed.
*
* A GROUP ACCOUNT IS REFUSED (10087).  A group account has no Windows user to
* put in a group; it is reached with LOGTO.
*
* THE ADMINISTRATOR REFUSALS ARE GONE, 18 Sep 26 - RELEASE_1.1 64.  The tiers
* are removed, so a Windows administrator's SD account is an ordinary account
* and sets its routes and its OS.USERS fields like any other; the only limit
* on what it can do is the one Windows imposes.  The 21 Aug / 27 Aug rules and
* the 18 Sep 58/62 posture this description used to record died with the tier
* that made the account an administrator.
*
* THE FOUR OS FORMS REFUSE A GROUP ACCOUNT (10105).  They are four switches
* over two fields rather than four names for one state, so SH-OFF leaves
* OS.EXECUTE alone.
*
* THE TWO REMOTE ROUTES, AND THERE IS NO THIRD.  The console and Remote Desktop
* are denied to every account SD creates - sdsshonly, applied by CREATE.ACCOUNT
* and enforced by gplbld/deny-logon.ps1 - and no verb here lifts that.  An
* administrator reaches the keyboard because Windows already calls them one,
* not because SD granted it.
*
* EACH IS ONE GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO ACCOUNTS.  sdssh is
* what sshd's AllowGroups names; sdapi is what APISRVR tests after the SCRAM
* proof succeeds.  A mirrored field could only ever disagree with the thing
* that actually decides - the reasoning that deleted ACC$USERS on 15 Aug.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $modify.account
$catalog $MODIFYA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h
* 
deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun os_group(action,group,member) calling '!os_group'
deffun is_group(group) calling '!is_group'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

   prompt ''

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end     

   parser = "!PARSER"

* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  WHAT REMAINS OF THE OLD BCOMP
*   BLOCK, after the tier machinery it existed for went with the tiers.
*   want.tier is the one state word the caller named - SUSPENDED or
*   UNSUSPENDED - and the route.* trio serve route.set, which is untouched.
   want.tier          = ''
   route.failed       = @false
   route.same         = @false
   route.stat         = 0

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors
   
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6000) ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
   end

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword) ;* Verb modify.Account

   * Get next token, account name
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      crt 'Command Syntax:  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADD | DELETE <username>'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH | API | BOTH | NONE'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-ON | SH-OFF'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON | OS-OFF'
      crt '                  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SUSPENDED | UNSUSPENDED'
      stop
   end
   *
   acc.name = upcase(acc.name)   ;* account names are always upper case
   if acc.name = 'SDSYS' then
      stop sysmsg(2202) ;* Account name is invalid
   end   
   *
   read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else      
      stop sysmsg(6003) ;* Account name is invalid
   end
   
   group.name = acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>   ;* get associated group
   
   if is_group(group.name) then
      * Get next token, ADD or DELETE
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - SSH, API, BOTH and NONE.  Owner's decision, 21 Aug
*   2026, replacing the SSH / NO.SSH / API / NO.API pairs of earlier the same
*   day.  Four actions in the slot ADD and DELETE occupy:
*
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH    ssh only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API    the API only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> BOTH   both
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NONE   neither
*
*   THE KEYWORD SAYS WHAT THE ACCESS IS, NOT WHAT TO CHANGE, and that is the
*   whole difference from the pairs it replaces.  "API" used to mean "add the
*   API to whatever it already had"; it now means "the API and nothing else",
*   so it WITHDRAWS ssh.  The same four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so an
*   account's access is said the same way whether it is being made or changed.
*
*   WHICH IS WHY THE MESSAGE STATES THE WHOLE RESULTING STATE rather than what
*   moved.  Someone who types API meaning BOTH has just taken ssh away, and a
*   message reading "may now use the API" would not tell them - it is true and
*   it is the wrong half.  10076-10079 each name both routes.
*
*   They take NO user name - they act on the account's OWN Windows user - so
*   they are handled before the ADD/DELETE test rather than inside it, which
*   would demand one.
*
*   THE PROPERTY IS GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING ELSE.  Nothing is written to
*   ACCOUNTS - owner's decision, 20 Aug 2026, kept - so there is no record that
*   could disagree with the group.
*
*   WRAPPED ROUND THE ADD/DELETE BLOCK RATHER THAN FOLDED INTO IT.  That block
*   is working code with four message paths in it; turning its "if ADD or
*   DELETE" into arms of a case statement would rewrite all of it to add
*   actions that share none of it.  One "if not(route.done)" leaves it
*   untouched.
      route.done = @true
      want.ssh = @false
      want.api = @false
      begin case
         case upcase(action) = 'SSH'
            want.ssh = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'API'
            want.api = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'BOTH'
            want.ssh = @true
            want.api = @true
            gosub route.set
         case upcase(action) = 'NONE'
            gosub route.set

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS, BOTH WAYS ROUND.  Owner,
*   27 Aug 2026: "sh-on and sh-off", "os-on and os-off".  CREATE.ACCOUNT takes
*   the two ON forms; the OFF forms are only meaningful once an account exists,
*   which is what this verb is for.
*
*   FOUR KEYWORDS OVER TWO FIELDS, NOT AN ABSOLUTE STATE.  SSH/API/BOTH/NONE
*   above name what the access IS and each one sets both groups; these are two
*   independent switches, so "sh-off" says nothing about OS.EXECUTE and leaves
*   it alone.  That is why os.set takes a field number rather than a pair of
*   wants.
         case upcase(action) = 'SH-ON'
            os.field = 1
            os.want  = @true
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'SH-OFF'
            os.field = 1
            os.want  = @false
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'OS-ON'
            os.field = 2
            os.want  = @true
            gosub os.set
         case upcase(action) = 'OS-OFF'
            os.field = 2
            os.want  = @false
            gosub os.set

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - owner, 27 Aug 2026: a fourth state, SUSPENDED, "so
*   that an account can be temporarily denied access".  RELEASE_1.1 64 keeps
*   it and removes the tiers it sat beside.
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE THREE TIER KEYWORDS ARE GONE,
*   AND THEY ARE DELETED RATHER THAN REFUSED BECAUSE OF THE RDPACCOUNT
*   PRECEDENT ABOVE.  A caller who still types standard, programmer or
*   administrator falls to `case 1` - the same unrecognised-token answer any
*   other dead keyword gets - which is the owner's ruling (a syntax error)
*   arrived at by the mechanism already in this file rather than by a new
*   refusal branch.  CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses its two outright because there the
*   keyword also had side effects (make.admin); here it had none.
*
*   WHAT IS LEFT IS WHAT THE DECISION LEAVES.  An account has ONE level, so
*   there is no tier to move it to and no direction to move in; SUSPENDED is
*   the only state change this verb still performs, and it keeps its name
*   because it IS a state rather than a rank.
         case upcase(action) = 'SUSPENDED'
            want.tier = 'SUSPENDED'
            gosub tier.set

*        18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  UNSUSPENDED IS NEW, AND IT IS
*        NOT A CONVENIENCE - WITHOUT IT SUSPEND BECOMES A ONE-WAY DOOR.
*        Until this change the ONLY way to lift a suspension was to name a
*        tier: "naming a tier on a suspended account lifts the suspension into
*        that tier", written directly above.  Refusing the three tier keywords
*        removes that route, so the lift needs a name of its own, and the
*        owner's ruling is to keep suspend AND unsuspend.
*
*        IT CLEARS THE FIELD RATHER THAN SETS A LEVEL, now that suspension has
*        its own field (ACC$SUSPENDED).  want.tier carries the keyword so the
*        messages can say what was asked for; tier.set writes ''.
         case upcase(action) = 'UNSUSPENDED'
            want.tier = 'UNSUSPENDED'
            gosub tier.set

         case 1
            route.done = @false
      end case

      if not(route.done) then
      if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') or (upcase(action) = 'DELETE') then
      *  valid action
      *   Get next token, user name
         call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, user.name, keyword) 
         if token.type = PARSER$END then
            stop sysmsg(10016)  ;* Missing User Name
         end
         
         if (upcase(action) = 'ADD') then

* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - THE TIER ORDERING APPLIES HERE TOO.  Owner's rule,
*   29 Aug 2026, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 57: a grant may go down or sideways, never
*   up.  This arm makes the identical os_group('ADDMEM', ...) call GRANT makes,
*   which is exactly why PRE_RELEASE 27 had to give it the same audit record.
*
*   RELEASE_1.1 64, 18 Sep 2026 - THE REFUSAL THAT USED TO STAND HERE IS GONE
*   WITH THE TIERS.  There is no ordering left for two verbs to disagree about.
*
*   DELETE IS NOT GUARDED AND NEVER WAS: taking access away is always allowed,
*   and refusing a revoke would strand exactly the memberships that want
*   removing.

            * Add user to group
            * does user belong to sdusers?
            if is_grp_member(user.name,'sdusers') then
            * this user looks legit, add to group
            * first check to see if user is already a member of the group
               if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
                  crt sysmsg(10017,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 is already a member of group %2
               end else
                  * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
                  * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
                  * if convert(os.name.chars, '', user.name) # '' ...
                  if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo usermod -aG ..."
                  stat = os_group("ADDMEM", group.name, user.name)
                  * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - AUDIT IT, the way GRANTA:208 does.  This arm makes
* the identical os_group('ADDMEM', ...) call that GRANT makes and wrote
* nothing, so who may enter an account could be changed by two verbs and only
* one of them left a trail.  AFTER the edit and inside "stat = 0", so it
* records what happened rather than what was attempted.  The verb is named in
* the text because the two routes are otherwise indistinguishable in the audit
* file.  The identity is stamped by audit_message() in k_error.c and is not
* passed from here, so a caller cannot forge it.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 27.
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD account=' : acc.name : ' to=' : user.name)
                     crt sysmsg(10018,user.name,group.name)  ;* %1 added to group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10019,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to add user %1 to group %s status: %2
                  end
                  end
               end 
            end else
               crt user.name
               crt sysmsg(10020,user.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
            end 
         
         end else
            * already tested action, must be delete
            if is_grp_member(user.name,group.name) THEN
               * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
               * Use shared !valid_os_name before os.execute (phase 2).
               if not(valid_os_name(user.name)) or not(valid_os_name(group.name)) then
                  crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,2) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
               end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: os.execute "sudo deluser ..."
               stat = os_group("DELMEM", group.name, user.name)
               * --------------------
                  if stat = 0 then
* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - and the DELETE arm, for the reason given at the ADD
* one above.  GRANTA:233 is the model.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 27.
                     void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT DELETE account=' : acc.name : ' from=' : user.name)
                     crt sysmsg(10021,user.name,group.name) ;*  %1 removed from group %2
                  end else
                     crt sysmsg(10022,user.name,group.name,stat) ;* Unable to remove user %1 from group %s status: %2
                  end
               end
            end else
               crt sysmsg(10023,user.name,group.name) ;* %1 is not a member of  %2
            end 
          
         end
         
         
      end else
         crt 'MODIFY.ACCOUNT Action Must Be ADD, DELETE, SSH, API, BOTH, NONE,'
         crt '   SH-ON, SH-OFF, OS-ON, OS-OFF, SUSPENDED or UNSUSPENDED'
      end
      end       ;* not(route.done)

   end else
     crt sysmsg(10024,group.name) ;* %1 is not a valid Windows group
   end
   goto program.end

* ======================================================================
* The account's OWN Windows user.
*
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - DERIVED FROM ACC$GROUP, NOT FROM THE RECORD ID.
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT downcases the user name (CREATEA) and writes the ACCOUNTS
*   record under the UPPER case name, so downcase(acc.name) would usually be
*   right - but "usually" is doing real work there and this decides whether
*   somebody can log in.  ACC$GROUP is "sdu_":acc.uname, written by
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT from the user name itself, so taking the name back out of it
*   is a reversal of what was actually recorded rather than a guess at it.
*
*   AND THE RESULT IS CHECKED AGAINST sdusers before anything acts on it, with
*   the same test the ADD path above already uses.  That is what rejects a
*   GROUP-type account: its ACC$GROUP is a name the caller supplied, not
*   "sdu_"-anything, so the name that comes out is not an SD user and the
*   check refuses it.  A GROUP account has no single Windows user for this to
*   mean anything about.
account.user:
   acc.user = ''
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdu_' then acc.user = group.name[5,999]
   if acc.user = '' then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.name) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
      user.ok = @false
      return
   end
   user.ok = is_grp_member(acc.user,'sdusers')
   if not(user.ok) then
      crt sysmsg(10020,acc.user) ;* %1 is not a member of sdusers, not allowed
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* SSH / API / BOTH / NONE - set this account's remote access to exactly what
* was asked for, by making its own Windows user's membership of sdssh and
* sdapi match want.ssh and want.api.
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - ONE ROUTINE, AND IT IS ABSOLUTE.  It replaced
*   route.add and route.remove, which each moved ONE group in ONE direction and
*   between them needed eight message numbers.  The keyword now says what the
*   access IS, so there is one operation - make the world match - and it is
*   idempotent by construction rather than by asking first.
*
*   IT REPORTS THE RESULTING STATE, NOT THE TRANSITION, for the reason given at
*   the case statement above: with an absolute keyword the interesting fact is
*   what the account can do now, and half of that may be something the caller
*   did not realise they were withdrawing.
*
*   "NOTHING CHANGED" IS STILL SAID SEPARATELY (10080).  Reporting the state
*   alone would leave a caller unable to tell a no-op from a change, and this
*   is a verb people run twice when they are not sure it took.
*
*   THE ADMINISTRATOR REFUSAL IS GONE, 18 Sep 26 - RELEASE_1.1 64.  The tiers
*   are removed, so a Windows administrator's SD account is an ordinary
*   account: it sets its routes like any other, and the only limit on what it
*   can do is the one Windows imposes.  What the 21 Aug / 27 Aug / 5 Sep /
*   18 Sep 58-62 refusals guarded no longer exists to guard.
*
*   NO sdsshonly GUARD, for the reason the routines this replaced gave: that
*   group carries SeDenyInteractiveLogonRight and no verb in this program
*   touches it.  sdssh cannot lock an administrator out of ssh either, because
*   allow-ssh-groups.ps1 names the Administrators group in AllowGroups in its
*   own right.  If a future route ever writes to sdsshonly, the guard returns.
*
*   THE WITHDRAWAL IS IMMEDIATE AND THE GRANT MAY NOT BE.  sshd re-reads its
*   AllowGroups per connection and APISRVR asks the SAM per login, so neither
*   consults a token; but a Windows access token fixes group membership at
*   sign-in, so an account GRANTED a route mid-session may still need to sign
*   out and back in before anything token-based agrees.
route.set:
   * A GROUP ACCOUNT HAS NO REMOTE ROUTE TO SET.  It has no Windows user at all
   * - CREATE.ACCOUNT's GROUP arm makes an sdg_ group and reaches neither
   * create_user() nor set_passwd() - and it is reached with LOGTO or an F
   * pointer.  Said plainly here, because account.user below would otherwise
   * answer "is not a member of sdusers", which is true and explains nothing.
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdg_' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10087, acc.name) ;* %1 is a group account and has no remote access
      return
   end

   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

   gosub route.apply

   if route.failed then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10081, acc.user, route.stat) ;* Unable to change remote access
      return
   end

   if route.same then
      crt sysmsg(10080, acc.user) ;* %1 already had that access; nothing changed
      return
   end

   gosub say.access
   return

* ======================================================================
* route.apply  -  make acc.user's sdssh and sdapi membership match want.ssh and
* want.api.  Sets route.failed, route.same and route.stat; prints nothing.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - SPLIT OUT OF route.set, unchanged, because tier.set
* needs the same three group moves and must NOT inherit route.set's guards.
* An administrator is refused by route.set (10083) and a tier change is the one
* operation where an account's administrator-ness is itself what is moving, so
* the guard has to sit above this and not inside it.
*
* IT PRINTS NOTHING, which is the reason it is a separate routine rather than a
* flag.  route.set reports the resulting state; tier.set reports the tier and
* would otherwise print two verdicts about one command.
route.apply:
   route.failed = @false
   route.same   = @false
   route.stat   = 0

   if not(valid_os_name(acc.user)) then
      route.failed = @true
      route.stat = 2
      return
   end

   has.ssh = is_grp_member(acc.user, 'sdssh')
   has.api = is_grp_member(acc.user, 'sdapi')

   if (has.ssh = want.ssh) and (has.api = want.api) then
      route.same = @true
      return
   end

   if has.ssh # want.ssh then
      if want.ssh then
         route.stat = os_group("ADDMEM", 'sdssh', acc.user)
      end else
         route.stat = os_group("DELMEM", 'sdssh', acc.user)
      end
   end

   * The API half runs only if the ssh half succeeded.  A failure part way
   * leaves the account in a state neither the caller nor this message
   * describes, so it is reported with the status and NOT summarised as though
   * it had worked.
   if route.stat = 0 then
      if has.api # want.api then
         if want.api then
            route.stat = os_group("ADDMEM", 'sdapi', acc.user)
         end else
            route.stat = os_group("DELMEM", 'sdapi', acc.user)
         end
      end
   end

   if route.stat # 0 then route.failed = @true
   return

* ======================================================================
* SH-ON / SH-OFF / OS-ON / OS-OFF - set ONE field of this account's own
* Windows user's OS.USERS record to yes or no.
*
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - owner, 27 Aug 2026.  os.field is 1 for SH - the SH
* verb and "!" at the prompt, read by CPROC - and 2 for OS.EX, read by
* op_sh.c's os_permitted() for OS.EXECUTE and by EDIT's check.permitted for the
* two screen editors.
*
* ***THE ADMINISTRATOR REFUSAL IS GONE WITH THE TIERS, 18 Sep 26 - RELEASE_1.1
* 64.***  A Windows administrator's SD account is an ordinary account: it can
* turn its own operating-system access on and off like any other, and the only
* limit on what it can do is the one Windows imposes.  The 27 Aug rule this
* banner records - that none of the three routes could be taken away from an
* administrator - died with the tier that made the account an administrator.
*
* A MISSING RECORD READS AS "no" IN BOTH FIELDS, exactly as both gates read it,
* so turning something off that was never on is "nothing changed" and writes
* nothing.  The only way to get a record that says no twice is to have had one
* say yes, which is a fact worth keeping rather than tidying away.
*
* READU AND RELEASE, because two administrators editing OS.USERS at once is
* rare and silent corruption of a permission list is not a thing to leave to
* chance.  The no-op path releases without writing.
os.set:
   if group.name[1,4] = 'sdg_' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10105, acc.name) ;* %1 is a group account and has no Windows user
      return
   end

   gosub account.user
   if not(user.ok) then return

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.f else
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user) ;* Unable to change operating-system access
      return
   end

   readu osu.rec from osu.f, acc.user else osu.rec = ''

   os.was = (upcase(trim(osu.rec<os.field>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES')
   if os.was = os.want then
      release osu.f, acc.user
      close osu.f
      crt sysmsg(10080, acc.user) ;* %1 already had that access; nothing changed
      return
   end

   os.new = 'no'
   if os.want then os.new = 'yes'
   osu.rec<os.field> = os.new

*  BOTH FIELDS ARE REWRITTEN, in the two spellings this program uses, so a
*  record hand-edited to "YES" or "Yes" or "y" comes out of here saying what
*  the gates will actually read.  The gates fold case and compare to "yes"; a
*  record that says "y" has always meant no and would otherwise keep looking
*  as though it meant something.
   osu.sh = 'no'
   osu.ex = 'no'
   if upcase(trim(osu.rec<1>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES' then osu.sh = 'yes'
   if upcase(trim(osu.rec<2>, ' ', 'B')) = 'YES' then osu.ex = 'yes'
   osu.rec = osu.sh
   osu.rec<2> = osu.ex

   osu.ok = @true
   write osu.rec to osu.f, acc.user on error osu.ok = @false
   close osu.f

* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - AND THE SAME FALLBACK CRED_SET HAS.  PRE_RELEASE 68.
*
*   os.users is READ-ONLY to sdusers - secure-osusers.ps1 grants (OI)(CI)(RX) -
*   so this write is refused by Windows for a session that reached SDSYS with
*   LOGTO from an unelevated start, however administrator SD believes it is
*   after CPROC:2769.  Same cause as the $cred write, same route out: ps_script
*   hands the work to the elevated helper when K$ADMINISTRATOR is set
*   (PS_SCRIPT:166) and falls back to os.execute when it is not, so an ordinary
*   caller behaves exactly as before.  The long version of the reasoning is in
*   CRED_SET, including why CRLF and why ASCII are both measured facts.
*
*   AFTER close, DELIBERATELY.  The readu above holds an update lock and close
*   releases it; letting an outside process write the file while SD holds a lock
*   on the record would be a lock SD believes in and Windows knows nothing about.
   if not(osu.ok) then
      osu.path = kernel(K$WINPATH, @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' : @ds : acc.user)
      if osu.path # '' then
         q = char(39)
         ps =       '$p = ' : q : osu.path : q
         ps := @fm : '$l = @(' : q : osu.sh : q : ',' : q : osu.ex : q : ')'
         ps := @fm : '$nl = [char]13 + [char]10'
         ps := @fm : 'try {'
         ps := @fm : '  [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, ($l -join $nl) + $nl,'
         ps := @fm : '     [System.Text.Encoding]::ASCII)'
         ps := @fm : '} catch { exit 1 }'
         ps := @fm : 'exit 0'

         if ps_script(ps) = 0 then osu.ok = @true
      end
   end

*  READ BACK BEFORE REPORTING, for CRED_SET's reason: the elevated path runs a
*  script whose exit code says the FILE was written and nothing about whether SD
*  can read it back, and this file is the whole of the OS.EXECUTE gate.
   if osu.ok then
      osu.ok = @false
      openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'os.users' to osu.chk then
         read osu.back from osu.chk, acc.user then
            if upcase(trim(osu.back<1>, ' ', 'B')) = upcase(osu.sh) then
               if upcase(trim(osu.back<2>, ' ', 'B')) = upcase(osu.ex) then
                  osu.ok = @true
               end
            end
         end
         close osu.chk
      end
   end

   if not(osu.ok) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10104, acc.user)
      return
   end

   crt sysmsg(10102, acc.user, osu.sh, osu.ex) ;* os.users: %1 has SH %2, OS.EXECUTE %3
   return

* ======================================================================
* SUSPENDED / UNSUSPENDED - set the account's suspension state.
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THIS IS ALL THAT IS LEFT OF THE OLD
*   tier.set, and it is all a suspension ever needed.  The tier machinery it
*   used to carry - Windows membership moves, the route and os.users blocks,
*   voc.delta and field 6 - went with the tiers; SYSCOM/KEYS.H says why field
*   6 is gone.  A suspension changes nothing on Windows and nothing in the
*   VOC, so the state write below is the whole operation.
*
* THE ORDER OF WORK IS CHOSEN FOR WHAT A FAILURE LEAVES BEHIND.  Every step
*   before the register write is a read, and the write is one field, so a run
*   that dies part way leaves the register saying the OLD state and re-running
*   the same command converges.
*
* NOTHING IS WITHDRAWN ON WINDOWS FOR A SUSPENSION, which is the owner's
*   ruling and is what makes lifting one free.  The stated cost is in LOGIN
*   beside the refusal: the ssh connection is still accepted and SD refuses
*   the login, rather than sshd refusing the connection.
tier.set:
   old.state = upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SUSPENDED>, ' ', 'B'))
   new.state = ''
   if want.tier = 'SUSPENDED' then new.state = 'SUSPENDED'

*  THE EQUALITY GUARD.  "suspended" on a suspended account and "unsuspended"
*  on any other both land here, so a no-op writes nothing and reports 10110.
   if old.state = new.state then
      crt sysmsg(10110, acc.name, want.tier) ;* %1 is already %2; nothing changed
      return
   end

*  DO NOT LET SOMEBODY SUSPEND THEIR WAY OUT OF THE BUILDING.  Both names are
*  tested because they can differ: @logname is the account you log in to and
*  the one this would lock, @who is the account you are standing in after a
*  LOGTO.  Suspending either from inside it leaves a session that works until
*  it ends and then cannot be re-established, and the only way back is
*  "sd -internal", which is undocumented and elevated.
   if want.tier = 'SUSPENDED' then
      if (acc.name = upcase(@logname)) or (acc.name = upcase(@who)) then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         crt sysmsg(10112) ;* You cannot suspend your own account or the one you are standing in
         return
      end
   end

*  ---- The register, last.  Readu, because two administrators editing one
*  ---- account at once is rare and silently lost state is not a thing to
*  ---- leave to chance.
   readu tier.rec.new from acc.f, acc.name else tier.rec.new = acc.rec

   tier.rec.new<ACC$SUSPENDED> = new.state

   tier.written = @true
   write tier.rec.new to acc.f, acc.name on error tier.written = @false

   if not(tier.written) then
      release acc.f, acc.name
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      crt sysmsg(10114, acc.name) ;* Unable to change the state of %1
      return
   end

   crt sysmsg(10109, acc.name, want.tier) ;* Account %1 is now %2

*  1 Sep 26 Windows port - SUSPENDED SAYS WHAT IT DID *AND WHAT IT DID NOT DO*.
*  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 111, owner's ruling 1 Sep 2026: "suspend an account and
*  all sd access is unavailable - nothing happens at the windows level is
*  correct - a message to the administrator to that effect might be good."
*
*  THE BOUNDARY IS EXACTLY THE THING PEOPLE GET WRONG.  Entry 20 stood for
*  months on the assumption that an account losing SD access must also lose its
*  Windows administrator rights.  If it read that way to the people who wrote
*  it, it reads that way to an administrator suspending somebody in a hurry.
*
*  EVERY CLAIM IN 10159 IS FROM THE CODE, not from the name of the state.  All
*  SD access goes: LOGIN covers local login AND ssh, since both arrive through
*  LOGIN; APISRVR covers the API; CPROC covers LOGTO.  Nothing on Windows
*  changes - entry 20's ruling, and it is correct.  The message must not imply
*  otherwise.
*
*  SUSPENDED ONLY.  Unsuspending obviously lifts access; there is nothing to
*  explain.
   if want.tier = 'SUSPENDED' then
      crt sysmsg(10159, acc.name)
      * what suspending took, what it left alone, and what lifts it
   end

   return

* ======================================================================
* Say what the account can reach now.  Shared with nothing today, but the same
* four messages are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, which is the point of them.
say.access:
   begin case
      case want.ssh and want.api
         crt sysmsg(10078, acc.user) ;* may sign in over ssh and use the API
      case want.ssh
         crt sysmsg(10076, acc.user) ;* ssh, and may not use the API
      case want.api
         crt sysmsg(10077, acc.user) ;* the API, and may not sign in over ssh
      case 1
         crt sysmsg(10079, acc.user) ;* no remote access at all
   end case

*  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 125: warn (non-prohibitively) when the access just set has
*  no active transport on this machine - the same heads-up create.account gives,
*  from the shared shape.  The grant persists and activates when the transport
*  comes on, so this is not a refusal.  Native checks only, no PowerShell probe.
   if want.ssh and not(ospath('C:\WINDOWS\System32\OpenSSH\sshd.exe', OS$EXISTS)) then
      crt sysmsg(10161, acc.user)  ;* set up for ssh, but no ssh server yet
   end
   if want.api and (config('APIPORT') + 0) <= 0 then
      crt sysmsg(10162, acc.user)  ;* set up for the API, but no listener yet
   end
   return

program.end:
   END
